//! Where ferrotape reads otherwise than the reference readers.
//!
//! ferrotape reads an archive as GNU tar, bsdtar, Go's `archive/tar` and
//! Python's `tarfile` read it, and refuses one they read differently. This
//! page lists every kind of archive on which ferrotape reads otherwise than
//! at least one of them: where they disagree among themselves, where
//! ferrotape refuses an archive one of them reads, and where it reads one
//! that one of them refuses. Each was measured with GNU tar 1.35 and Python
//! 3.12.3 as Ubuntu 24.04 packages them, with fixes their own releases lack,
//! and with bsdtar 3.8.11 and Go 1.27.0.
//!
//! Each row names the cases of the repository's catalog (`catalog/`) that
//! show it, each holding the archive, each reader's recorded reading, and
//! ferrotape's verdict in every setting. A row naming no case was measured by
//! extraction, by an attribute's value, or by the mode GNU tar or bsdtar
//! lists, which the catalog does not record. "Strict" and "Compatible" are
//! the two [`Level`](crate::Level)s; where a row names neither, both read
//! alike.
//!
//! Some Rust readers are counted at the Strict level alone; where only they
//! disagree, Compatible reads as the reference readers do.
//!
//! # Where an archive begins and ends
//!
//! | Archive | Reference readers | ferrotape | Cases |
//! | --- | --- | --- | --- |
//! | A stream that ends before its first header | GNU tar and Python: not an archive; bsdtar and Go: an empty archive | not a tar archive ([`NotATar`](crate::ErrorKind::NotATar)) | `framing/an-empty-stream` |
//! | A stream that ends after a pax header, before its member | GNU tar and Go: an empty archive; bsdtar and Python fail | refused ([`Truncated`](crate::ErrorKind::Truncated)) | `framing/a-stream-that-ends-after-metadata` |
//! | A stream that ends inside a member's data | each lists the member and fails, but Python, which fails first | the member, then refused when its data is read | `framing/a-stream-that-ends-inside-data` |
//! | A zero block, then a header | GNU tar, bsdtar and Python end the archive at the zero block; Go fails; GNU tar with `--ignore-zeros` reads on | Strict: refused ([`TrailingData`](crate::ErrorKind::TrailingData)); Compatible: the archive ends at the zero block | `framing/one-zero-block-then-a-member` |
//! | Anything but zeros after the end marker | each stops at the marker; GNU tar with `--ignore-zeros` reads on | never read as archive; Strict: refused by [`Reader::finish`](crate::Reader::finish) | `framing/a-member-after-the-end-marker` |
//!
//! # Header fields
//!
//! | Archive | Reference readers | ferrotape | Cases |
//! | --- | --- | --- | --- |
//! | A device number that does not parse, in any header with magic | bsdtar, Python and Go fail; GNU tar reads a regular file's, and a device's with a wrong number | refused ([`HeaderField`](crate::ErrorKind::HeaderField)) | `headers/a-regular-file-with-an-unreadable-device-number`, `headers/a-character-device-with-an-unreadable-device-number` |
//! | A device number that does not parse, in a v7 header or one under any other magic | Python reads every header's device fields, and ends the archive at one it cannot read, having listed the members before it, or refuses it where it is the first header; GNU tar and Go read on, and bsdtar after the first header | refused ([`HeaderField`](crate::ErrorKind::HeaderField)) | `headers/a-v7-header-with-an-unreadable-device-number`, `headers/a-v7-header-with-an-unreadable-device-number-first` |
//! | A numeric field holding digits, then a space and more, in its mode, ids, size, time or device numbers | GNU tar reads the first digits, and bsdtar where the header is not the archive's first; Go fails at the header; Python refuses the archive where it is the first header, and otherwise ends the archive there, having listed the members before it | refused ([`HeaderField`](crate::ErrorKind::HeaderField)) | `headers/a-size-of-digits-a-space-and-more-digits`, `headers/a-mode-of-digits-a-space-and-more-digits-after-a-member`, `headers/a-device-number-of-digits-a-space-and-more-digits-after-a-member` |
//! | A GNU header that is not typeflag `S` holding old GNU sparse slots (bytes 386 to 481), or, for a member that carries data, a real size (483 to 494) | bsdtar reads a regular file's real size as its size and fails on a slot; GNU tar, Go and Python read the header's own fields | refused ([`SparseFields`](crate::ErrorKind::SparseFields)); the extension flag alone, and a real size on a member that carries no data, are read as every reader reads them | `headers/a-gnu-regular-file-with-an-old-sparse-real-size`, `headers/a-gnu-regular-file-with-old-sparse-slots`, `headers/a-gnu-directory-with-old-sparse-slots` |
//! | A size of 2<sup>60</sup> or more, declared by a header or a pax `size` record | bsdtar stops at the member ("Tar entry size overflow"), listing only the members before it; GNU tar, Python and Go read a member that carries no data on past it, and a regular file until its data runs out, where Python fails at once | refused ([`HeaderField`](crate::ErrorKind::HeaderField), [`PaxValue`](crate::ErrorKind::PaxValue)); a size of 2<sup>60</sup> - 1 is read as any other | `headers/a-size-of-2-to-the-60-on-a-symbolic-link`, `headers/a-size-just-under-2-to-the-60-on-a-symbolic-link`, `pax/a-size-record-of-2-to-the-60` |
//! | A GNU or v7 header whose bytes at offset 345 begin with one that is not NUL, as in a GNU incremental dump | GNU tar, bsdtar and Go name the member from its name field; Python joins those bytes to it as a prefix | refused ([`PrefixArea`](crate::ErrorKind::PrefixArea)) | `headers/bytes-in-a-gnu-header-s-prefix-area`, `headers/bytes-in-a-v7-header-s-prefix-area` |
//! | A POSIX header that names its member with a prefix ending in `/` | GNU tar, Go and Python join the prefix and the name with a `/` (`a//b`); bsdtar adds none (`a/b`) | refused ([`PrefixSlash`](crate::ErrorKind::PrefixSlash)); read where a pax `path` or a long name replaces the name | |
//! | An empty path | GNU tar substitutes `.`; bsdtar skips the member and fails; Python and Go keep the empty name | refused ([`EmptyName`](crate::ErrorKind::EmptyName)) | `headers/an-empty-name`, `gnu/an-empty-long-name` |
//! | A header whose magic and version are neither POSIX's (`ustar` and a NUL, any version) nor GNU's (`ustar`, a space, then a space and a NUL), holding an owner name, or, for a device, a device number other than 0 | Python reads the names and device numbers under any magic, and owns what it extracts by the names; bsdtar reads them where the magic begins `ustar`; GNU tar and Go only under those two | refused ([`UnknownMagic`](crate::ErrorKind::UnknownMagic)); read where those fields hold nothing, a device's number 0,0 | `headers/an-unknown-magic-with-owner-names`, `headers/a-v7-header-with-owner-names`, `headers/a-character-device-under-gnu-magic-with-another-version` |
//!
//! # Types
//!
//! | Archive | Reference readers | ferrotape | Cases |
//! | --- | --- | --- | --- |
//! | A regular member (typeflag `0`, NUL or `7`) whose resolved path ends in `/` | GNU tar and bsdtar: a directory (GNU tar lists a `7` as a contiguous file, and extracts a directory); Python and Go: a file | a directory | `types/a-regular-file-named-with-a-trailing-slash`, `types/a-contiguous-file-named-with-a-trailing-slash`, `pax/a-path-ending-in-a-slash-over-a-regular-file`, `pax/a-binary-hdrcharset-beside-a-path-ending-in-a-slash`, `pax/a-utf-8-hdrcharset-beside-a-path-ending-in-a-slash` |
//! | A member of a typeflag GNU tar does not know (any but NUL, `0` to `7`, `D`, `K`, `L`, `M`, `S`, `V`, `X`, `g` and `x`) whose resolved path ends in `/` | bsdtar reads a directory, with no data; GNU tar and Python extract a regular file holding the body it declares, so where it declares one, the headers in it are members to bsdtar alone | refused ([`UnknownTypeSlash`](crate::ErrorKind::UnknownTypeSlash)) | `types/an-unknown-typeflag-named-as-a-directory`, `types/an-unknown-typeflag-named-as-a-directory-declaring-a-size`, `types/an-unknown-typeflag-named-as-a-directory-after-a-file` |
//! | The same, declaring data | bsdtar reads no data, and then reads the data as headers | refused ([`DirectoryWithData`](crate::ErrorKind::DirectoryWithData)) | `types/a-regular-file-named-with-a-trailing-slash-declaring-data` |
//! | The same, its pax header holding a record other than those ferrotape applies, `comment` and `hdrcharset` | bsdtar reads a regular file after a record it fails to parse (an access control list it cannot parse, a `LIBARCHIVE.creationtime` that is not a time, a `LIBARCHIVE.symlinktype` it does not know), and a directory otherwise; GNU tar reads a directory | refused ([`DirectoryWithRecord`](crate::ErrorKind::DirectoryWithRecord)) | `pax/an-unparseable-access-list-beside-a-path-ending-in-a-slash`, `pax/an-unparseable-default-list-beside-a-path-ending-in-a-slash`, `pax/an-unparseable-nfs4-list-beside-a-path-ending-in-a-slash`, `pax/a-creation-time-that-is-not-a-time-beside-a-path-ending-in-a-slash`, `pax/an-unknown-symlink-type-beside-a-path-ending-in-a-slash`, `pax/an-unparseable-access-list-over-a-file-named-with-a-trailing-slash`, `pax/an-access-list-beside-a-path-ending-in-a-slash`, `pax/a-device-number-record-beside-a-path-ending-in-a-slash` |
//! | GNU's dumpdir (typeflag `D`) | GNU tar and bsdtar: a directory; Python and Go: a member of an unknown type | a directory, its data read as data | `types/a-gnu-dumpdir` |
//! | A directory, a device or a FIFO whose header declares a size, which POSIX permits | each reads no data, and the headers in the declared body are members; some Rust readers read the body as data | Strict: refused ([`SizeWithoutData`](crate::ErrorKind::SizeWithoutData)); Compatible: read, with no data | `types/a-directory-declaring-a-size`, `types/a-character-device-declaring-a-size`, `types/a-block-device-declaring-a-size`, `types/a-fifo-declaring-a-size` |
//! | A symbolic link whose header declares a size, where POSIX requires zero | as for a directory | Strict: refused ([`SizeWithoutData`](crate::ErrorKind::SizeWithoutData)); Compatible: read, with no data | `types/a-symbolic-link-declaring-a-size` |
//! | A device, a FIFO or a symbolic link whose header declares a size, read by GNU tar 1.35 as GNU released it, without the fix for CVE-2026-5704 that the measured build carries | GNU's release takes the declared size as the member's body, so the members in it, which the measured build and the other readers list, are hidden | Strict: refused ([`SizeWithoutData`](crate::ErrorKind::SizeWithoutData)); Compatible: read, as the measured build reads it | `types/a-character-device-declaring-a-size`, `types/a-block-device-declaring-a-size`, `types/a-fifo-declaring-a-size`, `types/a-symbolic-link-declaring-a-size` |
//! | A hard link whose header declares a size, whose data POSIX's pax format says follows | each reads no data | refused ([`SizeWithoutData`](crate::ErrorKind::SizeWithoutData)) | `types/a-hard-link-declaring-a-size` |
//! | A link, a directory, a device or a FIFO whose size a pax `size` record declares | GNU tar reads that much data after it, and bsdtar too after a hard link; Python and Go read none, and the headers in the declared body are members to them | refused ([`SizeWithoutData`](crate::ErrorKind::SizeWithoutData)) | `pax/a-size-record-on-a-directory`, `pax/a-size-record-on-a-character-device`, `pax/a-size-record-on-a-fifo`, `pax/a-size-record-on-a-symbolic-link`, `pax/a-size-record-on-a-hard-link` |
//! | A hard link whose header names no target | GNU tar links it to `.`, the directory it extracts into; bsdtar, Python and Go read the empty name | refused ([`HardLinkWithoutTarget`](crate::ErrorKind::HardLinkWithoutTarget)) | `types/a-hard-link-with-no-target` |
//! | A member that is not a link, its link target stated by a GNU long link or a pax `linkpath` | bsdtar extracts it as a hard link to the target, writing the member's data into the target; GNU tar and Python make it as its kind | refused ([`TargetWithoutLink`](crate::ErrorKind::TargetWithoutLink)); a target in the header's own field is read | |
//! | A volume label (typeflag `V`), wherever it falls | GNU tar lists a volume header and extracts nothing; bsdtar neither lists nor extracts it; Python extracts a file; Go yields a member. After a pax header or a long name, bsdtar applies it to the member after the label, the others to the label | refused ([`VolumeLabel`](crate::ErrorKind::VolumeLabel)) | `types/a-volume-label`, `types/a-volume-label-declaring-a-size`, `types/a-pax-header-before-a-volume-label`, `types/a-long-name-before-a-volume-label` |
//! | A continuation from another volume (typeflag `M`) | each lists a member; on extraction, GNU tar creates nothing, and bsdtar and Python a file | refused ([`VolumeContinuation`](crate::ErrorKind::VolumeContinuation)) | `types/a-continuation-from-another-volume` |
//! | Solaris's extended header (typeflag `X`) | GNU tar, bsdtar and Python apply it to the next member; Go reads a file | refused ([`SolarisExtendedHeader`](crate::ErrorKind::SolarisExtendedHeader)) | `types/a-solaris-extended-header` |
//! | Solaris's access control list (typeflag `A`) | bsdtar reads it as metadata, not a member; the others as a member | refused ([`SolarisAcl`](crate::ErrorKind::SolarisAcl)) | `types/a-solaris-acl` |
//!
//! # Sparse files
//!
//! A sparse member is read where its map takes a shape every reader extracts
//! alike: see [`Sparse`](crate::Sparse) and [`SparseFlaw`](crate::SparseFlaw).
//! A real size of 2<sup>60</sup> or more is refused, as bsdtar fails at it
//! ([`RealSize`](crate::SparseFlaw::RealSize)); a map larger than
//! [`Limits::metadata`](crate::Limits::metadata), 1 MiB by default, which Go
//! refuses past the same size, is refused ([`MapLimit`](crate::SparseFlaw::MapLimit)).
//!
//! | Archive | Reference readers | ferrotape | Cases |
//! | --- | --- | --- | --- |
//! | A sparse file in GNU's pax format 0.0, 0.1 or 1.0, in the shapes GNU tar and bsdtar write | each reads the file alike; Rust's `tar` (Strict) reads a regular member of the stored data under the header's name, and in format 0.0 under the file's own | Compatible: the file; Strict: refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`PaxForm`](crate::SparseFlaw::PaxForm))) | `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-0.0`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-0.1`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-ending-in-data`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-beginning-with-an-empty-region`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-whose-map-runs-past-a-block`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-without-its-name`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-a-hole-of-one-byte`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-an-empty-region-where-a-region-ends`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-bytes-after-its-map`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-leading-zeros-in-its-map` |
//! | A map ending before the file's real size, with no empty region at it | GNU tar extracts the file only to the end of the last region, or empty where there is none; the others to the real size | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`End`](crate::SparseFlaw::End))) | `sparse/an-old-gnu-sparse-file-ending-in-a-hole-without-its-last-region`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-ending-in-a-hole-without-its-last-region`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-no-region`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-whose-last-region-is-empty-before-its-end`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-whose-count-is-less-than-its-regions` |
//! | Regions out of order, or overlapping | bsdtar and Go fail; GNU tar and Python extract different files | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`Order`](crate::SparseFlaw::Order))) | `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-regions-out-of-order`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-regions-overlapping` |
//! | A region holding data that begins where the last one holding data ended, empty regions between them or not | bsdtar fails, extracting nothing after it; the others read it | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`Adjacent`](crate::SparseFlaw::Adjacent))) | `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-regions-that-touch`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-regions-that-touch-across-an-empty-one` |
//! | A map leaving no hole in a file of a real size above zero | bsdtar fails; the others read it | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`NoHole`](crate::SparseFlaw::NoHole))) | `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-no-hole` |
//! | Regions whose lengths are not the data the member stores | Go fails after the member; GNU tar, bsdtar and Python extract different data, or GNU tar fails | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`StoredSize`](crate::SparseFlaw::StoredSize))) | `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-storing-more-than-its-regions`, `sparse/an-old-gnu-sparse-file-storing-less-than-its-regions` |
//! | A map number not in the form GNU tar writes: in format 1.0, more than 19 digits or a sign; in format 0.1, a letter | GNU tar fails on a format 1.0 one, where bsdtar or Go read it; each reads a 0.1 one otherwise | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`Number`](crate::SparseFlaw::Number))) | `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-a-map-number-of-twenty-digits`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-with-a-plus-in-its-map`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-0.1-whose-map-holds-a-letter` |
//! | A count of regions other than the regions given | Go refuses the archive; the others read it | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`Count`](crate::SparseFlaw::Count))) | `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-0.0-counting-more-regions-than-it-gives` |
//! | Sparse records not of exactly one form: a version other than 1.0, one of its two version records missing, or records of two forms | the readers read different files, or bsdtar fails | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`Records`](crate::SparseFlaw::Records))) | `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-2.0`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-without-its-major-version`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-beside-a-numblocks-record` |
//! | A sparse member named otherwise by `GNU.sparse.name` than by a `path` record or a GNU long name, named with a trailing slash, or by a name holding a NUL | GNU tar, bsdtar and Go take the sparse name, Python the later record or the long name; GNU tar and bsdtar extract a directory where the name ends in `/`, bsdtar alone for `GNU.sparse.name`; GNU tar and bsdtar cut a name at a NUL, where Go and Python keep it | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`Name`](crate::SparseFlaw::Name))) | `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-whose-name-and-path-differ`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-after-a-long-name`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-named-with-a-trailing-slash`, `sparse/an-old-gnu-sparse-file-named-with-a-trailing-slash`, `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-whose-name-holds-a-nul` |
//! | A pax `size` record on a sparse member | Python reads that many bytes as the file; the others read the map | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`SizeRecord`](crate::SparseFlaw::SizeRecord))) | `sparse/a-sparse-file-in-pax-format-1.0-beside-a-size-record` |
//! | Sparse records on a member whose typeflag is not `0` | bsdtar fails ("Non-regular file cannot be sparse"); GNU tar extracts the member as its kind, or a symbolic link as a regular file | refused ([`Sparse`](crate::ErrorKind::Sparse)([`NotRegular`](crate::SparseFlaw::NotRegular))) | `sparse/sparse-records-on-a-symbolic-link`, `sparse/sparse-records-on-a-contiguous-file` |
