//!
//! # Pax records
//!
//! | Archive | Reference readers | ferrotape | Cases |
//! | --- | --- | --- | --- |
//! | A pax extended header with no records | bsdtar fails there, extracting nothing after it; GNU tar, Python and Go read on | refused ([`EmptyMetadata`](crate::ErrorKind::EmptyMetadata)) | `pax/an-empty-pax-header` |
//! | A `hdrcharset` naming neither of POSIX's character sets, `BINARY` and `ISO-IR 10646 2000 UTF-8` | bsdtar reports an error and reads on, and reads a `path` ending in `/` over a regular file as a file, where GNU tar, which ignores the record, reads a directory | refused ([`UnknownCharset`](crate::ErrorKind::UnknownCharset)) | `pax/an-unknown-hdrcharset`, `pax/an-unknown-hdrcharset-beside-a-path-ending-in-a-slash`, `pax/a-lower-case-binary-hdrcharset`, `pax/an-empty-hdrcharset` |
//! | A second pax header, long name or long link before one member | GNU tar and Go take the last; Python the first, or both; bsdtar fails on a second pax header and reports a second long name as an error | refused ([`RepeatedMetadata`](crate::ErrorKind::RepeatedMetadata)) | `pax/two-pax-headers-before-a-member`, `gnu/two-long-names-before-a-member` |
//! | A keyword repeated in one pax header, its values differing | each takes the last, as POSIX specifies; some Rust readers take the first | Strict: refused ([`RepeatedKeyword`](crate::ErrorKind::RepeatedKeyword)); Compatible: the last | `pax/a-path-given-twice`, `pax/a-size-given-twice` |
//! | An empty value of a keyword ferrotape applies (`path`, `linkpath`, `uname`, `gname`, and the numeric and time keywords) | some read it as deleting the record, as POSIX specifies, and some as an empty value; GNU tar reports an empty number or time as malformed | refused ([`EmptyPaxValue`](crate::ErrorKind::EmptyPaxValue), [`PaxValue`](crate::ErrorKind::PaxValue)) | `pax/an-empty-path`, `pax/an-empty-link-target`, `pax/an-empty-owner-name`, `pax/an-empty-group-name`, `pax/an-empty-access-time`, `pax/an-empty-change-time`, `global/an-empty-uid-under-a-global-uid` |
//! | An `atime` or `ctime` that is not a time | GNU tar and bsdtar report it and read on; Go fails; Python reads it | refused ([`PaxValue`](crate::ErrorKind::PaxValue)) | `pax/an-access-time-that-is-not-a-time`, `pax/a-change-time-that-is-not-a-time` |
//! | A time before 1970 with a fraction | bsdtar reads `-1.5` as half a second before 1970; the others as one and a half | refused ([`FractionBeforeEpoch`](crate::ErrorKind::FractionBeforeEpoch)) | `pax/a-time-before-1970-with-a-fraction` |
//! | A time with a fraction finer than a microsecond | GNU tar, bsdtar and Go read it to the nanosecond; Python holds it as a floating-point number, which rounds finer than about 0.24 microseconds and reads `.9999999` and above as the next second | read to the nanosecond | |
